<p>Every type in Bayan: what it holds and its rules. Where the compiler rejects a misuse, the section shows the diagnostic code and its real message. Planned features appear as fragments.</p>

<h2 id="primitives">Primitive types</h2>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Type</th><th scope="col">What it is</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>i8 i16 i32 i64</code></td><td>Signed integers of 8, 16, 32, 64 bits.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>u8 u16 u32 u64</code></td><td>Unsigned integers.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>isize usize</code></td><td>Signed and unsigned integers the width of a pointer.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>f32 f64</code></td><td>Floating-point numbers.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>bool</code></td><td><code>true</code> or <code>false</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>rune</code></td><td>One Unicode scalar value, 32 bits wide.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>string</code></td><td>Valid UTF-8 text. See <a href="#strings">Strings</a>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>never</code></td><td>The type of an expression that does not return. See <a href="#never">never</a>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>dec</code></td><td>A decimal type for exact decimal arithmetic.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Setting <code>style.primitives</code> spells the integer and float types <code>int</code>, <code>long</code>, <code>double</code>; both spellings are the same type, and <code>bayan format</code> rewrites a project to its own. <code>char</code>, <code>decimal</code> and <code>object</code> have no equivalent.</p>
<note>Planned: <code>dec</code> has no literal yet (<code>1.50dec</code> is rejected with BYN2022). <code>style.primitives</code> comes from <code>package.byn</code> (see the <a href="ref-cli.html">command reference</a>).</note>

<h2 id="numbers">Number conversions and arithmetic</h2>
<p>Widening is implicit when no value can be lost (<code>i8</code> where <code>i64</code> is expected). Narrowing is a call, <code>u8(x)</code>, which panics in every build mode when the value does not fit; <code>u8.tryFrom(x)</code> gives <code>u8?</code>, <code>null</code> when it does not fit.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Operators</th><th scope="col">On overflow</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>+ - *</code></td><td>Stop the program in every build mode.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>+% -% *%</code></td><td>Wrap around at the type's size.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>+| -| *|</code></td><td>Stop at the largest or smallest value.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Division by zero also stops the program; integer division rounds toward zero, float-to-integer conversion drops fractions.</p>
<example name="ref-types-primitives"/>
<note>Planned: <code>x.truncate&lt;u8&gt;()</code>, <code>bitcast&lt;T&gt;(x)</code> and <code>checked(expr)</code> are specified, not in the compiler.</note>

<h2 id="strings">Strings</h2>
<p>A <code>string</code> is immutable UTF-8 and a view of bytes: a literal, a slice, or text it owns.</p>
<ul>
  <li><code>s.length</code> is the byte count and costs nothing; <code>s.runeCount</code> reads the whole string to count runes.</li>
  <li>Offsets are bytes: <code>s[a..b]</code> slices by bytes and panics if an end falls inside a character.</li>
  <li>There is no <code>s[i]</code> (BYN2201): read a byte with <code>s.bytes[i]</code>, a slice with <code>s[a..b]</code>, or characters with <code>s.runes.take(n)</code>.</li>
  <li><code>contains</code>, <code>startsWith</code> and <code>endsWith</code> return <code>bool</code>; <code>position</code> an <code>i64?</code> byte position.</li>
  <li><code>parts(by:)</code> and <code>lines</code> split lazily into views without copying; <code>lowercased()</code>, <code>uppercased()</code> and <code>replaced(_, by:)</code> return and allocate new text.</li>
  <li><code>s.copy()</code> makes an independent copy, so a borrowed string outlives its source.</li>
  <li><code>==</code> compares bytes with no ambient locale; there is no <code>+</code> (BYN2209), so join with <code>$"{a}{b}"</code>.</li>
</ul>
<example name="ref-types-strings"/>
<pre><code class="byn">let s = "abc"
print($"{s[1]}")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2201:</span> a `string` cannot be indexed
  --&gt; str-index.byn:2:11

<span class="gut">  2 │</span> print($"{s[1]}")
<span class="gut">    │</span>           <span class="hl">^^^ strings have no indexer</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
  help: read a byte, slice, or take characters; `string` is UTF-8, so position 1 is not a character:
          `s.bytes[1]`       the byte at offset 1 (applied below)
          `s[a..b]`          the text between byte offsets a and b
          `s.runes.take(n)`  the first n runes
          `s.graphemes`      user-perceived characters
<span class="gut">    │</span> 2 │ print($"{s.bytes[1]}")
<span class="gut">    │</span>   │           ++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler’s message for this program.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2 id="symbols">Symbols</h2>
<p><code>Symbol</code> is an interned name: a 4-byte value that compares by identity in constant time and orders by text. A string literal where a <code>Symbol</code> is expected becomes one at build time; any other string needs <code>Symbol.intern(text)</code>, which allocates and keeps the text until the program ends (BYN2832). <code>sym.text</code> is the string back; <code>$"{sym}"</code> writes it. There is no integer conversion.</p>
<example name="ref-types-symbol"/>

<h2 id="units">Units and distinct types</h2>
<p>A distinct type wraps an existing type so values of different meaning cannot mix: <code>type distinct UserId = i64</code> does not convert to or from <code>i64</code>. Build with <code>UserId(7)</code>, read <code>.value</code>. A bare literal never becomes one: <code>let id: UserId = 0</code> is BYN3204, so write <code>UserId(0)</code>. Distinct types compare only with their own, and a <code>requires</code> rule can make building fail (see <a href="ref-statements.html#requires">Statements</a>).</p>
<example name="ref-types-distinct"/>
<pre><code class="byn">type distinct Qty = i32 requires it &gt; 0

let q: Qty = 0</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN3204:</span> `0` is not a `Qty`
  --&gt; union-lit.byn:3:14

<span class="gut">  3 │</span> let q: Qty = 0
<span class="gut">    │</span>              <span class="hl">^</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
  help: wrap it: `Qty(0)`
<span class="gut">    │</span> 3 │ let q: Qty = Qty(0)
<span class="gut">    │</span>   │              ++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler’s message for this program.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>A <em>unit</em> is a distinct type with arithmetic and a literal suffix:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">unit Seconds(s): i64
unit Millis(ms) = Seconds / 1000
unit Bytes(B): i64
unit KiB = 1024 B

let t = 2s + 500ms          // Millis 2500
let billed: Minutes = duration.ceiling()</code></pre>
<note>Planned: units are not in the compiler; a <code>unit</code> line is a syntax error. Mixing units is an error. A value converts without a cast only to a finer unit (<code>2s + 500ms</code>); coarser needs a named rounding, <code>t.floor&lt;Seconds&gt;()</code>, <code>t.ceiling&lt;Seconds&gt;()</code> or <code>t.rounded&lt;Seconds&gt;()</code> (BYN3205 if omitted). A bare literal never takes a unit, <code>0</code> included (BYN3203). Units vanish at run time and print as value and symbol (<code>524288 B</code>); counts such as <code>s.length</code> stay plain <code>i64</code>.</note>

<h2 id="collections">Arrays, spans and collections</h2>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Type</th><th scope="col">Holds</th><th scope="col">Writable</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>T[N]</code></td><td>A fixed-size array, stored inline.</td><td>Yes.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Span&lt;T&gt;</code></td><td>A borrowed view of a run of elements.</td><td>Yes.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>ReadOnlySpan&lt;T&gt;</code></td><td>A borrowed view.</td><td>No. A <code>Span&lt;T&gt;</code> converts to it; the other direction never converts.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>List&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>Map&lt;K, V&gt;</code></td><td>Owned elements; they allocate.</td><td>Yes.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>
<p>A compile-time index out of range is an error (BYN2213); <code>xs[^1]</code> counts from the end and <code>xs[1..3]</code> spans positions 1 and 2. A <code>List</code> or array becomes a span as an argument; elsewhere <code>xs.span</code> is a read-only view.</p>
<p><code>xs.remove(from: a, until: b)</code> removes positions <code>a</code> up to but not including <code>b</code> and keeps the rest in order; <code>through:</code> includes the end. A bad range panics.</p>
<ul>
  <li><code>Map</code> keeps insertion order and is the only hash map; <code>HashMap</code> is rejected with a fix (BYN2830).</li>
  <li>A <code>Map</code> key is an integer, <code>rune</code>, <code>bool</code>, <code>string</code>, plain enum, <code>Symbol</code>, or a tuple or struct of keys; anything else is BYN2760, a non-key struct or tuple BYN2831.</li>
  <li>Hashing is seeded by default against hash-flooding.</li>
  <li><code>List</code>, <code>Map</code> and <code>StrBuf</code> are <code>unique</code>: assigning one moves it (see <a href="#unique">Move-only types</a>); copy with <code>xs.copy()</code>.</li>
</ul>
<example name="ref-types-collections"/>
<pre><code class="byn">let scores: Map&lt;f64, i64&gt; = []</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2760:</span> `f64` cannot be a `Map` key
  --&gt; map-key.byn:1:17

<span class="gut">  1 │</span> let scores: Map&lt;f64, i64&gt; = []
<span class="gut">    │</span>                 <span class="hl">^^^</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
  help: use an integer type, `rune`, `bool`, `string`, `Symbol`, a plain enum, or a tuple or struct
        of those as the key</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler’s message for this program.</figcaption>
</figure>
<pre><code class="byn">let grid: i32[3] = [1, 2, 3]
print($"{grid[7]}")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2213:</span> index 7 is out of range for `i32[3]`
  --&gt; const-index.byn:2:15

<span class="gut">  2 │</span> print($"{grid[7]}")
<span class="gut">    │</span>               <span class="hl">^</span></pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler’s message for this program.</figcaption>
</figure>
<note>Planned: <code>Set&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>Deque&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>Pool&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>Ring&lt;T, N&gt;</code>, <code>NonEmpty&lt;T&gt;</code> and <code>EnumMap</code> are specified, but the compiler reports <code>unknown type</code>. Typed indices (<code>List&lt;T, RowId&gt;</code> with <code>type distinct RowId = Index</code>), a module-level <code>const</code> array in read-only data and <code>StrBuf.over(stackalloc …)</code> are not in the compiler either.</note>

<h2 id="optionals">Optionals</h2>
<p><code>T?</code> is a <code>T</code> or <code>null</code>; plain types are never null.</p>
<ul>
  <li><code>x is null</code> and <code>x is not null</code> test it; after <code>is not null</code>, a <code>let</code> or parameter has its non-null type.</li>
  <li><code>x ?? fallback</code> gives the fallback when <code>x</code> is null.</li>
  <li><code>x ?? return v</code>, <code>?? break</code>, <code>?? continue</code> leave early and <code>?? fail .e</code> fails; they bind the non-null value (<code>let at = find(xs, at: 7) ?? return -1</code>).</li>
  <li><code>x == null</code> is rejected with a fix to <code>x is null</code> (BYN2027).</li>
</ul>
<example name="ref-types-optionals"/>
<note>Planned: safe navigation <code>?.</code> is specified, not in the compiler.</note>

<h2 id="tuples">Tuples</h2>
<p>A tuple groups values: <code>(1, "a")</code>. A named element is reachable with a dot: <code>(i32 count, string label)</code> as a type, <code>p.count</code> as an access. A tuple pattern unpacks one in <code>switch</code>, <code>is</code> and a <code>for</code> loop head.</p>
<example name="ref-types-tuples"/>
<note>The compiler does not unpack a tuple in a <code>let</code> (<code>let (a, b) = t</code> is a syntax error) and has no <code>t.0</code>; name the elements or use a pattern.</note>

<h2 id="results">Results</h2>
<p><code>Result&lt;T, E&gt;</code> holds a <code>T</code> or an <code>E</code> in the same storage as a tag. A <code>fails E</code> function returns one under the hood, and you can name the type yourself. <code>r.isOk</code> tells which, <code>r.value</code> reads the value, <code>r.err</code> the error, and a value can be returned where a <code>Result</code> is expected. Formatting prints the value or an error's summary line. More in <a href="errors.html">Errors</a>.</p>
<example name="ref-types-result"/>

<h2 id="never">never</h2>
<p><code>never</code> is the type of an expression that does not come back: <code>panic</code>, <code>exit</code>, a loop with no exit. It fits wherever a value is expected: a <code>catch</code>, after <code>??</code>, a <code>switch</code> arm; code after one is unreachable.</p>
<example name="ref-types-never"/>

<h2 id="structs">Structs</h2>
<p>A struct is a value with named fields, the only type with fields; no classes, no inheritance.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Type first.</strong> Fields, parameters and return types put the type first; a local puts the name first (<code>let w: Grams = 0g</code>). <code>Grams w = 0g</code> is BYN1043 with a fix.</li>
  <li><strong>Defaults and literals.</strong> <code>i32 y = 0</code> defaults a field, which <code>Point { x: 1 }</code> may omit; a <code>requires</code> rule is checked when built. A type is <em>defaultable</em>, creatable empty, only if every field has one.</li>
  <li><strong>Computed members.</strong> <code>i32 sum =&gt; x + y</code> is a property: no arguments, read not called.</li>
  <li><strong>Methods are read-only by default.</strong> A mutating method declares <code>ref this</code> first, or BYN2115 with a fix; it cannot be called on a <code>let</code> (BYN2104).</li>
  <li><strong>Equality</strong> and hashing go field by field; a struct is a <code>Map</code> key when all its fields are.</li>
  <li><strong>No default display.</strong> A struct in a <code>$"{…}"</code> hole needs <code>formatInto</code> or <code>{x, as: .debug}</code>, else BYN2225.</li>
  <li><strong>Field writes.</strong> A <code>requires</code> rule, <code>states</code> block or <code>@sealed</code> allows writes only from <code>ref this</code> methods or <code>with</code> (BYN3216); plain data allows direct writes.</li>
</ul>
<example name="ref-types-struct"/>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Counter {
    i32 n
    void bump() {
        n += 1
    }
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2115:</span> `bump` modifies `this` but is not declared `ref this`
  --&gt; no-ref-this.byn:4:9

<span class="gut">  4 │</span>         n += 1
<span class="gut">    │</span>         <span class="hl">^ `this` is modified here</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
  help: declare it `ref this` as the first parameter
<span class="gut">    │</span> 3 │     void bump(ref this) {
<span class="gut">    │</span>   │               ++++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler’s message for this program.</figcaption>
</figure>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Counter {
    i32 n
    void bump(ref this) {
        n += 1
    }
}

let c = Counter { n: 0 }
c.bump()</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2104:</span> `c` is immutable
  --&gt; let-mutate.byn:9:1

<span class="gut">  9 │</span> c.bump()
<span class="gut">    │</span> <span class="hl">^ `bump` modifies it; declared with `let` on line 8</span>
