<h3 id="constructors">Constructors and required fields</h3>
<p>A constructor is a function named after the struct; once declared, call it to build, and a literal is BYN2111. A <code>required</code> field must be set by the end of construction.</p>
<example name="ref-types-constructor"/>

<h3 id="struct-planned">Embedding</h3>
<note>Planned: <code>@noEquality</code> (removes <code>==</code>) is unknown (BYN2770); <code>embed T</code>, which promotes an embedded struct's fields and methods, is not in the compiler (BYN1004).</note>

<h2 id="unique">Move-only types</h2>
<p><code>struct unique</code> declares a type that cannot be copied: one owner, cleaned up once when it goes away. Use it for files and locks.</p>
<ul>
  <li>A type with a <code>unique</code> part is <code>unique</code>: a struct with a <code>List</code> field, a union with a <code>unique</code> payload, a tuple, array or optional of one, or a struct holding <code>Span&lt;T&gt;</code> (a mutable borrow).</li>
  <li>A <code>unique</code> value is never copied by accident: <code>let g = f</code> is BYN2780; fix with <code>move f</code>, an explicit copy (<code>f.copy(to: alloc)</code>) or <code>ref</code>.</li>
  <li>A <code>unique</code> parameter takes <code>move</code> to give ownership; a read-only one borrows.</li>
  <li>Once moved, the source cannot be used; a <code>unique</code> type cannot be <code>const</code> (BYN3209).</li>
</ul>
<example name="ref-types-unique"/>
<pre><code class="byn">struct unique File {
    i32 fd
}

let f = File { fd: 3 }
let g = f
print($"{g.fd}")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2780:</span> implicit copy of a unique value: `File` is declared `unique struct`
  --&gt; implicit-copy.byn:6:9

<span class="gut">  6 │</span> let g = f
<span class="gut">    │</span>         <span class="hl">^</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
  help: hand the value over with `move f`; pass it by `ref` to share it instead
<span class="gut">    │</span> 6 │ let g = move f
<span class="gut">    │</span>   │         +++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler’s message for this program.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>The full ownership rules are in <a href="ref-memory.html">Memory model</a>.</p>

<h2 id="unions">Unions</h2>
<p>A union is one of several cases, each possibly with named data, stored inline as a tag plus room for the largest case, no allocation.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Case names are camelCase, one per line; a one-line union is rejected (BYN1053).</li>
  <li>Every payload is named, <code>rect(i32 w, i32 h)</code>; an unnamed one is BYN1064.</li>
  <li>A single-payload case may drop its label (<code>.percentOff(10)</code>); several need labels: <code>.rect(w: 3, h: 4)</code>.</li>
  <li>A leading dot (<code>.rect(…)</code>) names the case expected there; <code>Shape.circle(2)</code> names it in full.</li>
  <li>A <code>switch</code> must cover every case, and the error names the missing one (BYN2410); a <code>_</code> arm hides later additions and is a lint warning on a union from the same package.</li>
  <li>Read a payload by pattern: <code>.circle c</code> binds the case as <code>c</code>; <code>.rect(let w, let h)</code> by position.</li>
  <li>A union is closed: no integer cast. A payload-free case may carry one for C, <code>read = 1</code>, unique inside the union.</li>
</ul>
<example name="ref-types-union"/>
<pre><code class="byn">union Shape {
    circle(i32 r)
    empty
}

i32 area(Shape s) =&gt; s switch {
    .circle c =&gt; c.r,
}
print($"{area(.empty)}")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2410:</span> `switch` does not handle every `Shape`
  --&gt; missing-case.byn:6:22

<span class="gut">  6 │</span> i32 area(Shape s) =&gt; s switch {
<span class="gut">    │</span>                      <span class="hl">^ not covered: .empty</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
  help: add the missing arms: .empty</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler’s message for this program.</figcaption>
</figure>
<pre><code class="byn">union Shape {
    circle(i32)
    empty
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1064:</span> payload `i32` of `.circle` needs a name
  --&gt; payload-name.byn:2:12

<span class="gut">  2 │</span>     circle(i32)
<span class="gut">    │</span>            <span class="hl">^^^ no name</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
  help: name the payload: `.circle(i32 name)`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler’s message for this program.</figcaption>
</figure>
<note>Planned: union members after the cases, and <code>T.tryParse(text)</code> for a union, are not in the compiler.</note>

<h2 id="enums">Enums</h2>
<p>An enum is a closed set of named values with constant data; the value is a small number, the data in read-only tables.</p>
<ul>
  <li>A value's data is listed in the declaration: <code>mercury(3, 24)</code> for <code>enum Planet(i64 mass, i64 radius)</code>.</li>
  <li>The compiler generates <code>name</code>, <code>ordinal</code>, <code>values</code>, <code>count</code>, <code>parse(text)</code> and <code>fromOrdinal(n)</code>; the last two return an optional. <code>count</code> today is <code>length</code> in the specification.</li>
  <li>There is no integer cast; <code>fromOrdinal</code> is the checked way.</li>
  <li>An enum <code>switch</code> must cover every value, like a union.</li>
</ul>
<example name="ref-types-enum"/>
<note>Planned: enum members (<code>public f64 surfaceGravity =&gt; …</code> after the values) are a syntax error; <code>enum open</code> (unknown values, C interop) and <code>extensible</code> (callers supply a fallback) are not in the compiler.</note>

<h2 id="error-sets">Error sets</h2>
<p><code>union error</code> declares a set of failures, with or without data, in the union grammar, used only in <code>fails</code> clauses and <code>catch switch</code>; <code>fails</code>, <code>try</code> and <code>catch</code> are in <a href="ref-expressions.html#catch">Expressions</a> and <a href="errors.html">Errors</a>.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Compose</strong> with <code>=</code> and commas: <code>union error ShipError = CarrierError, StockError</code> takes all their cases, and a part converts to the whole implicitly.</li>
  <li><strong>Narrow</strong> with <code>except</code>: <code>union error Retryable = CarrierError except .rejected</code>.</li>
  <li>Two parts sharing a case name are an error (BYN3202); a case name belongs to its set, so <code>EmailError.invalid</code> and <code>UrlError.invalid</code> differ and an ambiguous <code>.invalid</code> is an error.</li>
  <li>A doc comment on a case is its message; <code>$"{e}"</code> prints its first line (see <a href="ref-lexical.html#doc-comments">Doc comments</a>).</li>
  <li><code>IoError</code> and <code>Utf8Error</code> are built in.</li>
</ul>
<example name="ref-types-errors"/>

<h2 id="interfaces">Interfaces</h2>
<p>An interface is a set of members a type promises to have.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Conformance is explicit (<code>struct Square : IShape</code>); interface names begin with <code>I</code>.</li>
  <li>A member with a body is a default method, copied into each conforming type.</li>
  <li>A property requirement is a bare <code>Type name</code>. One that changes the receiver has <code>ref this</code>, and so must every implementation.</li>
  <li>A <code>uses</code> clause is an upper bound; an implementation may use fewer effects.</li>
  <li>As a generic constraint (<code>where T : IShape</code>) the call resolves at build time, at no run-time cost.</li>
  <li>As <code>dyn ref IShape</code> the call goes through a table: a <code>dyn ref</code> parameter needs a <code>var</code> argument, and <code>dyn ref readonly I</code> allows a <code>let</code> but only members without <code>ref this</code> (BYN2813). An interface name alone as a type is BYN2816.</li>
  <li>Only structs conform (BYN2815 for unions and enums).</li>
</ul>
<example name="ref-types-interface"/>
<note>Planned: retroactive conformance (<code>extension Money : IJsonEncode</code>), <code>static abstract</code> members and generic interface methods are not in the compiler.</note>

<h2 id="function-types">Function types</h2>
<p><code>A -&gt; B</code> is a function type from <code>A</code> to <code>B</code>; <code>(A, B) -&gt; C</code> takes two, <code>() -&gt; void</code> none. After the return type, <code>uses e</code> adds effects, <code>fails E</code> an error set. <code>-&gt;</code> is for types, <code>=&gt;</code> for lambdas and bodies.</p>
<ul>
  <li>A function type before a name needs parentheses (<code>by (i32 -&gt; i32) f</code>); an alias avoids them: <code>type Pred = i32 -&gt; bool</code>, then <code>by Pred p</code>.</li>
  <li>Two function types are equal when parameters, return type, error sets and effects are equal; no variance.</li>
  <li>A <em>named function</em> works as a value when it is non-generic, module-level and its signature matches exactly; particles are part of the name, not the type. A mismatch is BYN2202; a method or generic function used as a value is BYN2824.</li>
  <li>A <em>lambda</em> is <code>x =&gt; …</code>, <code>(a, b) =&gt; …</code> or <code>() =&gt; …</code>, its bare parameters typed from context; an argument mentioning <code>it</code> where a one-parameter function is expected is a lambda with that parameter.</li>
  <li>A lambda copies the <code>let</code> values it reads when created; it cannot capture a <code>var</code> (BYN2821) or a <code>unique</code> value.</li>
  <li>A function value borrows the frame that made it; it may be passed down but not stored or returned.</li>
  <li>A function type may be a parameter, alias target or local; as a field it is BYN2822.</li>
</ul>
<example name="ref-types-function"/>
<pre><code class="byn">type Pricer = i64 -&gt; i64

struct Shop {
    Pricer pricer
}</code></pre>
