<h2 id="states">Typestate</h2>
<p>A struct can declare the states it passes through; the state is part of its type, so wrong-state use is a compile error at no run-time cost, and layout stays a tag plus payload.</p>
<ul>
  <li>States sit in one <code>states { … }</code> block after the fields, one per line with an optional named payload; at most one block per struct.</li>
  <li><code>Order.paid</code> is an <code>Order</code> known to be <code>paid</code>, valid anywhere a type is; <code>Order</code> alone means any state.</li>
  <li>A literal names its state (<code>Order.draft { id: 7 }</code>); plain <code>Order { … }</code> is BYN7005.</li>
  <li>Receiver state: <code>this is .paid</code>, <code>ref this is .draft</code>, <code>this is .paid or .shipped</code>.</li>
  <li>A payload is readable only when the type fixes the state (BYN7002); with several states, a field needs the same type in each.</li>
  <li><code>becomes .paid</code> declares a transition after the parameters; <code>become .paid(amount)</code> performs it. Only a <code>ref this</code> method may declare it, every normal return must <code>become</code>, and a failed call leaves the state unchanged. A <code>var</code> that becomes has the new state in the rest of the scope.</li>
  <li>Widening is free (<code>Order.paid</code> where <code>Order</code> is expected); narrowing uses <code>is</code> or <code>switch</code>.</li>
  <li>A <code>switch</code> over the states must cover each one; a new state errors at every switch missing it (BYN7004).</li>
</ul>
<example name="ref-types-typestate"/>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Order {
    i32 id
    states {
        draft
        paid(i32 payment)
    }
    void pay(ref this is .draft, i32 amount) becomes .paid {
        become .paid(amount)
    }
}

var o = Order.draft { id: 1 }
o.pay(5)
o.pay(5)</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN7001:</span> `pay` needs `o` to be `.draft`, but it is `Order.paid`
  --&gt; state-wrong.byn:14:1

<span class="gut">  14 │</span> o.pay(5)
<span class="gut">     │</span> <span class="hl">^</span>
<span class="gut">     │</span>
